    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d844">

                <pb facs="0345" n="1395" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v11p2/pg1395"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">110.11 DU/6–1153: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Afghanistan</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ward</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Kabul</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-06-11T12:00:00+04:30">June 11, 1953—noon.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>700. Foreign Minister states Washington Chargé<anchor n="1" xml:id="d844-f1"/>
                    indicates US Government “unkind” to Afghanistan in that no mention this country
                    made any public statement Secretary State Dulles re recent trip,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d844-f2"/> notwithstanding Iran mentioned which too not visited.</p>
                <pb facs="0346" n="1396" xml:id="pg1396"/>
                <p>He inquired whether I have received message for him, alleging Secretary State
                    informed Najibullah (Damascus telegram 1 May 15, repeated Department 698)<anchor
                        n="3" xml:id="d844-f3"/> that after discussions Karachi statement would be
                    sent me for delivery GOA.</p>
                <p>I again suggested sending Ambassador to Karachi, which evoked another impassioned
                    harangue on Pushtoonistan and intransigency Pakistan, but which ended
                    encouragingly for first time my experience in Foreign Minister statement
                    “perhaps it can be done, we shall see”. Toward end our conversation I spoke
                    metaphorically as Afghanistan Ambassador Karachi being drop of water which may
                    wear down the stone of Pakistan obduracy toward Pushtoon discussions, whereupon
                    Foreign Minister stated “we want Americans with pneumatic drill”.</p>
                <p>It may be Foreign Minister hopeful we would pressure GOP into Pushtoon
                    discussions as <hi rendition="#italic">quid pro quo</hi> for Pakistan wheat
                    loan, but I doubt his being so naive. More likely he apprehensive some
                    settlement Kashmir dispute possible, with lessened likelihood Pushtoon
                    discussions.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Ward</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d844-f1" n="1" xml:id="d844-f1-content">
                <p>Mohammad Hashim Khan Maiwandwal.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d844-f2" n="2" xml:id="d844-f2-content">
                <p>On May 9, Secretary of State John Foster Dulles and Director for Mutual Security
                    Harold E. Stassen embarked on a 20-day trip to the Near East and South Asia.
                    They visited India and Pakistan in South Asia but did not visit Afghanistan.
                    Najibullah Khan, the Afghan Ambassador to India, discussed the Pushtunistan
                    question with the Secretary and Assistant Secretary Byroade in New Delhi on May
                    19. No memoranda of these discussions have been found in Department of State
                    files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d844-f3" n="3" xml:id="d844-f3-content">
                <p>In telegram 1 from Damascus to Kabul, Ambassador Ward was informed that the
                    Secretary and Byroade would repeat the U.S. position on Pushtunistan to
                    Najibullah in New Delhi. (110.11 DU/5–1553)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
